rsc - pictures
==============

tray.svg is the mark that goes in the panel. Replace it with the real thing
and run make; nothing else has to change.

Two things it has to be:

  square, because a panel gives it a square and will squash anything else;

  readable on a pale panel and on a dark one. There is no way to ask a panel
  which it is, so a shape in one strong colour is the safe answer - fine light
  lines disappear against one of them.

What happens to it
------------------

icon-to-c.py rasterises it at the sizes a panel asks for and writes
tray-icon.h, which is compiled into tosaesd. So the picture is in the program
rather than beside it.

That is deliberate. An icon looked for at runtime has to be found at runtime -
an install prefix, a theme directory, a path relative to the binary - and one
that is not found leaves the item rendering as nothing, which looks exactly
like a session that failed to start. There is no way to tell those apart from
outside. A picture that is compiled in cannot go missing.

tray-icon.h is generated and committed, so an ordinary build needs no
rasteriser. Only changing the picture does, and any of rsvg-convert,
ImageMagick or Inkscape will do it.

Do not edit tray-icon.h. Edit the picture.
